  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 112 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nAutumn announces itself in a big way through the next week, with \nmuch milder conditions and a prolonged dry spell under the influence \nof two strong areas of surface high pressure.\n\nDry weather is forecast throughout the next seven days due to the \nexpansive influence of the aforementioned surface high pressure. \nBroadly cyclonic flow will be in place over the region, though any \nforcing shortwave troughs pivoting through this flow will be \ndisplaced well to the north, leaving  the primary impact to the area \nto be much milder temperatures through the period.\n\nSome moderation of temperatures is expected mid to late next week, \nwith 80 degree readings likely pushing back into portions of the \nregion, but substantially less humid conditions than experienced in \nrecent weeks. This leaves the primary weather hazard of concern as \nfire conditions, with afternoon relative humidity values likely to \ndrop into the 30 percent range on many if not most days, and \npossibly even lower as blended guidance often struggles to fully \ncapture the degree of mixing out of dewpoints in the afternoons with \nplentiful sunshine, even in settings with relatively light surface \nflow.\n\nAdditionally, there is at least a low chance for patchy frost mainly \nnortheast Monday night into Tuesday morning as lows will have the \npotential to drop into the upper 30s. Current numbers appear too \nwarm to support anything widespread or significant, but if we can \ndrop a couple/few more degrees in the outlying areas this may be on \nthe table.\n\nIn any case, several days of pleasant Autumn weather are expected \nover the next week, and all are encouraged to enjoy the abrupt shift \ninto mild fall days and cool nights.",
